          "inlines": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Give an example of an equation where using logarithmic laws loses solutions. Similarly, give an example where using them still gives all solutions.",
              "marks": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "For example, $\\log_3(x^2)=4$ has solutions $x=\\pm9$, but replacing it with $2\\log_3(x)=4$ loses $x=-9$.\n\nFor example, $\\log_2(x^3)=6$ and $3\\log_2(x)=6$ both have the single solution $x=4$.",
      "worked": "An example losing a solution is $\\log_3(x^2)=4$:\n$$\\begin{align*}x^2&=3^4\\\\x&=\\pm9\\end{align*}$$\nApplying the power law as $2\\log_3(x)=4$ instead gives\n$$\\begin{align*}\\log_3(x)&=2\\\\x&=9\\end{align*}$$\nThe original domain is $x\\ne0$, whereas $\\log_3(x)$ requires $x>0$, so the negative solution is lost.\n\nAn example retaining all solutions is $\\log_2(x^3)=6$. Its domain is $x>0$, which is also the domain after applying the power law:\n$$\\begin{align*}3\\log_2(x)&=6\\\\\\log_2(x)&=2\\\\x&=4\\end{align*}$$\nThe domains agree, so no solution is lost.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 116
  },
